{
  "genes": [
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Search a reflection-symmetric family containing three circles on the horizontal midline and nine mirrored pairs. Optimize representative centres, radii, and rectangle width with height equal to two minus width. Initialize independently generated quotient layouts, not only perturbations of the incumbent; finally release symmetry and polish each promising result."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Perform coordinated contact surgery: select three to six active contact or wall constraints, grouping reflected partners together. Preserve remaining contacts approximately while generating finite displacements that open the selected constraints. Permit temporary objective loss, then restore all nonoverlap inequalities and reoptimize. Compare against single-contact releases under equal computational budgets."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Generate cold starts from unequal row populations summing to 21, including alternating dense and sparse rows. Enumerate four through seven rows, vary horizontal offsets and row spacings, and initialize at least two radius classes. Optimize every layout with free centres and radii rather than preserving its row structure."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Mutate a packing by removing two or three circles and reinserting the same number into different large-clearance regions. Estimate clearance on a coarse grid using minimum wall distance and distance to each remaining disk. Jointly relax inserted circles and their neighbours before a full optimization, allowing temporary score loss during relocation."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Maintain a population indexed by contact-graph signature and aspect-ratio bin, retaining the best feasible packing in each niche. Allocate mutations across niches rather than selecting only the highest score. Use contact graphs canonicalized against circle relabeling and rectangle reflections, with a fixed geometric contact threshold, to suppress repeated exploration of the incumbent basin."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Eliminate radii and bounding-box variables through a fixed-centre linear program, then optimize its optimal value over centres. Derive centre sensitivities from contact and wall dual multipliers, verify their signs by finite differences, and use trust-region steps with LP reevaluation. Shrink steps when the active LP basis changes or predicted improvement fails."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Polish feasible layouts using sequential linear programs over centres, radii, and rectangle dimensions. For each pair, freeze its current unit separation vector and impose projected separation at least the radius sum. These conservative linear inequalities ensure geometric nonoverlap. Add centre trust bounds, maximize total radius, and update separation directions after accepted steps."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Replace escalating squared penalties with an augmented-Lagrangian inequality solver using analytic derivatives. Update nonnegative multipliers from constraint violations and increase the penalty only when feasibility stagnates. Bound radii strictly above zero and rectangle width away from zero and two. Finish promising solutions with a constrained second-order solver rather than further penalty escalation."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Explore aspect-ratio continuation instead of unconstrained local restarts: fix rectangle width on a sweep, affinely deform centres from the preceding solution, and reoptimize radii and centres. Sweep in both directions with multiple initial families. When the contact graph changes, branch a new trajectory; periodically release width and optimize the full problem."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Separate optimization from export certification. Refine promising active-contact systems at high precision, round to float64, then validate using the exact verifier arithmetic. Repair with tiny radius reductions and, if necessary, uniform scaling with explicit perimeter headroom. Adapt repair size to actual violations and require the final verified score gain, not the unrounded gain, to exceed 1e-10."
    }
  ]
}
